  "body": "Fixes #40.\n\nThe hold-off already exists and its intent is exactly right — the problem is one condition applied in the wrong branch.\n\n## The defect\n\n`mining_should_wait` applies the `MAX_CATCHUP_WAIT_SECS` escape **after** it has established that a peer is both fresh and ahead:\n\n```rust\nif best_peer_height <= our_height {\n    return None;\n}\nif now.saturating_sub(behind_since) > MAX_CATCHUP_WAIT_SECS {\n    return None;                      // <-- fires during a normal sync\n}\nSome(best_peer_height - our_height)\n```\n\nThat escape exists so an **isolated** node is not bricked. But isolation is already decided above, by `peer_fresh`. Reaching this point means a live peer confirmed a greater height inside `PEER_HEIGHT_TTL_SECS` — the node is syncing, not alone.\n\n`MAX_CATCHUP_WAIT_SECS` is **600**. Every initial sync of a real chain takes far longer than ten minutes, so the window always expires mid-sync and mining starts regardless of how far behind the node is.\n\nAnd because the node mines against **its own stale tip's difficulty**, those blocks are easy to find. It mines a private chain — precisely what the function's own doc comment and the `mining_loop` call site both warn about:\n\n> Every block mined while behind a peer is built on a tip that peer has already moved past, so it lands on a branch that loses the moment the two reconcile. The work is not merely wasted, it actively deepens a fork\n\n## Observed on mainnet, 1.0.5\n\nA node resyncing from genesis mined block 94,080 **eleven minutes after receiving that height from a peer**, while still 196,000 blocks behind:\n\n```\n06:47:47  extended +128 from 162.19.84.16:17891   ->  blocks=94080\n06:58:46  mined block 94080 difficulty=137542\n```\n\n**1,491 blocks were mined during that single resync.** The node finished `stalled_on_fork`, rejecting every peer with `unexpected height` (2,071 occurrences), and only a full chain wipe recovered it — which, with `--mine` still set, simply forked again. That loop is in #40.\n\n## The change\n\nDrop the escape from the fresh-and-ahead branch. Nothing else moves.\n\nThe isolation case is still covered: if the peer genuinely goes away, its height ages past `PEER_HEIGHT_TTL_SECS`, `peer_fresh` turns false, and the escape in that branch releases mining exactly as intended.\n\n## Tests\n\n```\na_fresh_ahead_peer_holds_mining_however_long_the_sync_takes\nan_ahead_peer_going_stale_still_releases_mining\n```\n\nThe first **fails on the unfixed code** rather than merely passing on the fixed one:\n\n```\nassertion `left == right` failed: a node with a fresh peer 195,920 blocks ahead must keep waiting\n  left: None\n right: Some(195920)\n```\n\nIt also asserts the hold still holds a day later — a long sync is not evidence of isolation.\n\nThe second pins the behaviour the escape was written for, so this does not trade one failure mode for another.\n\n```\nreorg_fetch       20 passed before  ->  21 passed after\nfull workspace    221 passed, 0 failed\n```\n\n`mining_should_wait` is pure and was already documented as testable without a node, which made this straightforward to cover.\n\n## Note for operators on 1.0.5\n\nUntil this lands, sync with mining **off**, then enable it at the tip:\n\n```\nnightfalld run --network mainnet --datadir ~/nfdata          # sync only\nnightfalld run --network mainnet --datadir ~/nfdata --mine   # once tip == best_peer_height\n```\n\nConfirmed working — with mining off the same node synced cleanly past the heights where it previously wedged, and incidentally **4.4x faster** (22 blocks/sec vs 5), since mining was also competing for the cores doing PoW verification.\n",
